.TH DWM 1 dwm\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dwm \- dynamic window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dwm
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
dwm is a dynamic window manager for X. It manages windows in tiled, monocle
and floating layouts. Either layout can be applied dynamically, optimising the
environment for the application in use and the task performed.
.P
In tiled layouts windows are managed in a master and stacking area. The master
area on the left contains one window by default, and the stacking area on the
right contains all other windows. The number of master area windows can be
adjusted from zero to an arbitrary number. In monocle layout all windows are
maximised to the screen size. In floating layout windows can be resized and
moved freely. Dialog windows are always managed floating, regardless of the
layout applied.
.P
Windows are grouped by tags. Each window can be tagged with one or multiple
tags. Selecting certain tags displays all windows with these tags.
.P
The status bar is drawn by the external panel (quickshell), which reports its
geometry and struts to dwm; dwm only reserves the workarea for it and can
toggle it.
.P
dwm draws a small border around windows to indicate the focus state.
.P
On start, dwm runs
.I scripts/autostart.sh
as a background process and never waits for it. On exit it runs
.I scripts/autostop.sh
if one exists, waiting up to 5 seconds so helpers started at login can shut
down cleanly. Both are searched for in the directories listed under FILES;
either may be omitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stderr, then exits.
.SH USAGE
.SS Status bar
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-b
Toggle the panel (status bar) on and off.
.P
The panel is owned by quickshell: it sets its own geometry, struts and mouse
handling. dwm only reserves the workarea for it.
.SS Keyboard commands
All keybindings are defined in
.I hotkeys.toml
(see CUSTOMIZATION) and are reloaded live when that file changes. The shipped
defaults are:
.TP
.B Super\-Return
Start the configured terminal (alacritty by default).
.TP
.B Super\-r / Super\-F1
Open the Quickshell app launcher / command menu.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-r
Restart Quickshell.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-c / Super\-Shift\-u
Toggle the control center / update center.
.TP
.B Super\-q
Close focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-j
Focus next window.
.TP
.B Super\-k
Focus previous window.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-j / Super\-Shift\-k
Move focused window down / up in the stack.
.TP
.B Super\-i
Increase number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B Super\-d
Decrease number of windows in master area.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-h / Super\-Control\-l
Decrease / increase master area size.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-h / Super\-Shift\-l
Increase / decrease column factor (cfact).
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-o
Reset column factor.
.TP
.B Super\-t
Sets tiled layout.
.TP
.B Super\-f
Sets floating layout.
.TP
.B Super\-m
Sets monocle layout.
.TP
.B Super\-space
Toggle between current and previous layout.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-space
Toggle focused window between tiled and floating state.
.TP
.B Super\-Tab
Toggles to the previously selected tags.
.TP
.B Super\-[1..n]
View all windows with nth tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-[1..n]
Add/remove all windows with nth tag to/from the view.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-[1..n]
Apply nth tag to focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-Control\-Shift\-[1..n]
Add/remove nth tag to/from focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-0
View all windows with any tag.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-0
Apply all tags to focused window.
.TP
.B Super\-comma / Super\-period
Focus previous / next screen, if any.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-comma / Super\-Shift\-period
Send focused window to previous / next screen, if any.
.TP
.B Super\-l
Lock the screen.
.TP
.B Super\-Shift\-e
Quit dwm.
.P
Media keys (volume, brightness, playback) and further launcher shortcuts
(wifi, bluetooth, weather, screenshots, wallpaper, ...) are also defined in
hotkeys.toml.
.SS Mouse commands
.TP
.B Super\-Button1
Move the focused window while dragging. Floating windows are moved freely;
tiled windows are placed interactively into the tiling layout.
.TP
.B Super\-Button2
Toggles focused window between floating and tiled state.
.TP
.B Super\-Button3
Resize focused window while dragging. Tiled windows are toggled to the
floating state.
.SH FILES
The scripts to be run along with dwm are searched for in the following
directories:
.IP "1. $XDG_DATA_HOME/dwm"
($HOME/.local/share/dwm by default)
.IP "2. $HOME/.dwm"
.IP "3. $DWM_PATH"
(the build directory baked in at compile time)
.P
Each directory is tried in order for
.IR scripts/autostart.sh ;
the first executable match is used.
.TP 15
scripts/autostart.sh
This file is started as a shell background process before dwm enters its
handler loop; dwm never waits for it.
.TP 15
scripts/autostop.sh
Optional and not shipped with dwm: if a user provides it, it is started after
the X connection is closed and dwm waits up to 5 seconds for it, so helpers
started by autostart.sh can be stopped cleanly.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
Runtime configuration lives in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dwm (~/.config/dwm by
default): hotkeys.toml defines key and mouse bindings, window-rules.toml
per-window rules, themes.toml colors and border width. dwm watches these
files and reloads them live on change; a manual reload can be requested with
.B kill \-USR1 $(pidof dwm).
If a file is missing there, dwm falls back to the installed copy in
$XDG_DATA_HOME/dwm/config and then to the copy shipped in the build tree.
Compile-time behavior (layouts, defaults, feature toggles) is customized by
editing config.h and recompiling.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xsetroot (1)
.SH ISSUES
Java applications which use the XToolkit/XAWT backend may draw grey windows
only. The XToolkit/XAWT backend breaks ICCCM-compliance in recent JDK 1.5 and early
JDK 1.6 versions, because it assumes a reparenting window manager. Possible workarounds
are using JDK 1.4 (which doesn't contain the XToolkit/XAWT backend) or setting the
environment variable
.BR AWT_TOOLKIT=MToolkit
(to use the older Motif backend instead) or running
.B xprop -root -f _NET_WM_NAME 32a -set _NET_WM_NAME LG3D
or
.B wmname LG3D
(to pretend that a non-reparenting window manager is running that the
XToolkit/XAWT backend can recognize) or when using OpenJDK setting the environment variable
.BR _JAVA_AWT_WM_NONREPARENTING=1 .
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at the project repository's issue tracker.
